 you know  languages perhaps hindi  uh  so that's what i got to look forward to  now how did i ever get there in the  first place so 2007 was the first video  but my life working life has been first  of all seven years as a diplomat and  then from 2000 from  basically  1974 uh  i was in the lumber business initially  working for large corporations and then  i set up my own company  and so that's been like 30 some odd  years that i've been in the uh  you know 40 years oh wow  that i've been in the in the lumber  field so why do i get into language it's  a long story um  i think around the year 2000 i thought  gee it would be good to really get a  hold of cantonese i kind of said a few  things like nehoma or whatever but i  wanted to learn to speak it properly and  so i discovered that there was a thing  called a minidisc player which to me was  amazing many disc player and online  uh dictionaries  but it started with a minidis player so  i've got people  to record stuff in cantonese for which i  had the text witness which i knew how to  read in mandarin  and so i started listening a bunch to  these minidisc  minidiscs that i had and then i started  uh recording the radio and they have  these cantonese talk shows here in  vancouver and so eventually i started uh  listening to cantonese radio  occasionally phoning in and speaking in  cantonese and i discovered that there  was a chinese immigrant  who had come to vancouver and had all  his money stolen all his life savings  stolen at the airport  and since my company at that time was  involved in in creating  software programs for sawmills which we  were selling in scandinavia and the  baltic states  uh we said well we'll give this guy a  chance you know we'll hire him if he's  good that's great for him and great for  us if he isn't very good then at least  we helped him out  and gave him a chance to work you know  in an environment here pick up some  english and stuff and we discovered that  his english was a real problem and so we  devised a program for him  based on how what i will always would  have liked to have had for my learning  you know learning german and spanish and  whatnot and that is to say um some  system for easily uh looking at words  and phrases saving them reviewing them  and if possible tying it to audio and so  that was kind of in a nutshell and so we  developed a system for learning english  which we called the linguist  um  so that's where my involvement with this  whole language thing began  um  to promote the linguist i wrote a book  called the linguist  which was uh written in 2002 two thou  published in 2003  um  in my videos of course i referred to  myself as the linguist which got  everybody all upset that is those people  who uh study linguistics they say you  can't call yourself a linguist and of  course dictionary definition makes it  very clear that for most  english-speaking people  and this is going back hundreds of years  someone who speaks a variety of  languages is known as a linguist  polyglot has become perhaps a more  popular term amongst people who who know  what the term means but i still believe  that a majority of native speakers of  english don't know what the what the  term polyglot means but i'm more  comfortable using the term now in fact  there's a whole community of polyglots i  go to conferences and so on and so on  but i wrote the book then to promote our  site the linguist  and  i also published it in chinese and in  and also in taiwan by the way and went  to china twice gave speeches to  universities uh in china  uh we had about ten thousand uh sold  about ten thousand copies of the book  each in china and in taiwan  tried to interest the immigration  department here in canada  and um  they wouldn't because they're more  interested in how they can acquire more  funding from the government not  necessarily in how they can help  immigrants learn english  so in the end we decided that we would  change it from an english only system to  a multi-language platform  and look for you know learners all over  the world and that's where link  developed and i can't tell you i can't  remember the date when the linguist  became linked the whole thing has been a  very lengthy process  lots of trial and error  uh you know it's been a long road but  what we have today is just so much  better than what we had way back then  it's it's it's not funny  um  so  there you have it um  10 years  look forward to learning more languages  for another 10 or 20 years however many  years i have  we are continuing to improve link  i hope that all of you  continue to pursue your interest in  languages whether it be one language  that you're trying to learn  or more than one language  i can assure you that the first one is  the most difficult because you have to  you have to persuade yourself that you  are capable  of transforming yourself into a person  who can speak another language  comfortably fluently  and i  i'll i'll digress a bit because i gave i  i did a video in french and i said uh  a monavie instead of i said  instead of  of course in french we say  so there was great discussion about how  can you possibly make such a mistake if  you claim to be  level c2 in french  i just want to emphasize that no matter  how good you are in a language you're  always going to make mistakes  and i know people who  i would say master english who have a  vocabulary  far better than the average native  speaker but they occasionally will make  a mistake it may be a habit that is  very deeply ingrained that they  consistently make as a mistake or it may  be a mistake that occasionally slips in  to their language we should not be  afraid of making mistakes  mistakes are how we learn  if we aren't making mistakes we aren't  challenging ourselves enough  so just a digression there  um  10 years 100 000 subscribers thank you  very much  um  [Music]  if you have any comments suggestions  please feel free i think i might often  might also put a uh a blog post up  uh on this subject  uh and there you have it i look forward  to the next 10 years  thank you for listening bye  [Music]  you

